# Line feeds in the working tree, on every system.
#
# Without this, git on Windows checks the tree out with CRLF, and two things
# that are the same file stop being the same file: `amberedit.cfg.example` is
# compiled into the binary as bytes and also read off disk by the tests, and the
# themes and the message template are compared against what the code holds. A
# checkout that rewrote their line endings made the embedded copy 1396 bytes
# longer than the rendered one — one per line — and the tests said so.
#
# It is also what the formats want. A config, a theme and a keymap are read by
# code that splits on \n and would carry a \r into every value; the test bases
# under testdata/ are binary and are marked so below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte for byte, whatever the platform: message bases, compiled nodelists and
# echolists, archives and the sample artwork are formats with their own layout,
# and a line ending is data there rather than punctuation.
*.sqd binary
*.sqi binary
*.sql binary
*.jhr binary
*.jdx binary
*.jdt binary
*.jlr binary
*.msg binary
*.zip binary
*.gif binary
*.png binary
# The zipped nodelist and echolist, whose extensions are the counters they
# are numbered by rather than anything a pattern above would catch.
testdata/nodelist/Z2PNT.Z19 binary
